@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+# Hegel property-based testing report: datafusion (datafusion-common)
+
+Target: `datafusion-common` (leaf member of the apache/datafusion workspace, pinned at 1c8295c).
+Feasibility: full workspace build was out of budget, but `datafusion-common` alone builds fast
+(~55 s cold test binary, ~6-30 s incremental), so all work was scoped with `-p datafusion-common`.
+The `rust-toolchain.toml` pin (1.97.0) auto-installed via rustup. `hegeltest 0.28.2` added as a
+dev-dependency with no friction.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+14 hegel tests (13 properties + 1 deterministic bug pin), added to the existing in-module test
+modules:
+
+`datafusion/common/src/scalar/mod.rs` — built around a `PrimSpec`/`draw_prim_value` generator
+covering 36 `ScalarValue` kinds (all ints, floats drawn from raw bits so NaN payloads/-0.0/
+infinities are reachable, utf8/binary in all three width variants, dates, times, timestamps with
+drawn timezones, durations, all three interval types, valid `Decimal128` precision/scale/value
+combinations, and `Null`), with ~20% nulls everywhere:
+
+1. `prop_scalar_eq_ord_hash_consistency` — manual `PartialEq`/`PartialOrd`/`Hash` impls are
+   mutually consistent: reflexivity (incl. bitwise NaN==NaN), antisymmetry of `partial_cmp`,
+   `eq` implies `Some(Equal)` + equal hashes, `Some(Equal)` implies `eq` within one data type,
+   and different variants are never equal/ordered.
+2. `prop_scalar_to_array_of_size_roundtrip` — `to_array_of_size(n)` yields the right length and
+   `DataType`, and every index round-trips through `try_from_array` back to the scalar.
+3. `prop_scalar_iter_to_array_roundtrip` — `iter_to_array` over same-typed scalars (mixed
+   null/non-null) reads back element-for-element via `try_from_array`.
+4. `prop_scalar_add_checked_matches_native_checked_add` — for all 8 integer kinds incl. nulls:
+   agrees with native `checked_add`, errors exactly on overflow, propagates nulls.
+5. `prop_scalar_sub_checked_matches_native_checked_sub` — same for `sub_checked`/`checked_sub`.
+6. `prop_scalar_add_wrapping_matches_native_wrapping_add` — the wrapping `add` (which has a
+   scalar fast path guarded by `can_use_direct_add` plus an arrow-kernel fallback) agrees with
+   native `wrapping_add`.
+
+`datafusion/common/src/rounding.rs` — floats drawn from raw bits with boundary bit patterns
+boosted (zeros, tiniest subnormals, MAX/MIN, infinities, NaN):
+
+7. `prop_next_up_next_down_match_std` — `next_up`/`next_down` agree bit-for-bit with std's
+   `f64::next_up`/`next_down` (and f32) everywhere except the deliberate signed-zero stepping
+   (`-0.0 -> +0.0 -> TINY`), which is asserted exactly.
+8. `prop_next_up_next_down_are_inverses` — `next_up` is the strict successor in the IEEE total
+   order and `next_down` its exact bitwise inverse (and vice versa), for both f32 and f64.
+
+`datafusion/common/src/table_reference.rs`:
+
+9. `prop_table_reference_quoted_string_roundtrip` — a `TableReference` built from arbitrary
+   non-empty parts (plain identifiers, arbitrary unicode text, and strings dense in `"`/`.`)
+   round-trips through `to_quoted_string` + `parse_str`.
+10. `prop_table_reference_empty_table_part_roundtrip` — KNOWN FAILURE pin, see Suspected bugs.
+
+`datafusion/common/src/stats.rs`:
+
+11. `prop_precision_usize_add_oracle` — `Precision::<usize>::add` is commutative, computes the
+    saturating sum, is `Exact` iff both inputs exact and no native overflow, `Absent` if either
+    input absent.
+12. `prop_precision_usize_sub_oracle` — same shape for `sub` (saturating difference, exactness
+    lost on underflow).
+13. `prop_precision_usize_multiply_oracle` — same shape for `multiply`, with small values
+    boosted so the non-saturating path is exercised too.
+14. `prop_precision_usize_min_max_consistency` — `min`/`max` agree with `std::cmp` on values,
+    are commutative, propagate exactness only when both inputs are exact, and satisfy
+    `min <= max`.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Default run (`cargo test -p datafusion-common --lib prop_`): 13/14 pass; the pinned
+  known-failure test fails deterministically on every run with the minimal counterexample
+  (`Partial { schema: "a", table: "" }` -> `"a."` -> `Bare { table: "a" }`).
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on all 14 tests): identical outcome — no additional
+  failures surfaced; reverted to the default case count afterwards.
+- All-features run (`--all-features`, which switches `TableReference` to the sqlparser-based
+  identifier parser): identical outcome (the pinned failure reproduces there too, with the
+  sql-feature fallback result `Bare { table: "a." }`).
+- Full crate suite (`cargo test -p datafusion-common --lib`): 524 passed, 1 failed (the pin).
+- Liveness verified: temporarily inverting the reflexivity assertion produced a fully shrunk
+  hegel counterexample (`Boolean(NULL)`); reverted.
+- `cargo fmt` clean; `cargo clippy -p datafusion-common --all-targets --all-features
+  -- -D warnings` clean. (The repo's pre-commit guidance asks for *workspace-wide* clippy; that
+  full build exceeds the time budget for this huge workspace, so clippy was scoped to the
+  touched crate. The single pre-existing `unused_imports` warning in `config.rs` tests is not
+  from this work.)
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**Empty identifier parts do not survive `to_quoted_string` -> `parse_str`** (pinned by
+`prop_table_reference_empty_table_part_roundtrip`, deliberately left failing):
+
+- `TableReference::partial("a", "")` (constructible; the constructors document "*NO*
+  normalization" and accept any string) serializes via `to_quoted_string()` to `"a."`.
+- Root cause: `quote_identifier("")` in `datafusion/common/src/utils/mod.rs` returns the empty
+  string *unquoted* — `needs_quotes("")` is `false` because the first-char check is skipped for
+  empty input and `chars.all(...)` is vacuously true. An empty identifier can only round-trip
+  if quoted as `""`.
+- Without the `sql` feature, `parse_identifiers` then drops the trailing empty segment
+  (`"a."` -> `["a"]` -> `Bare { table: "a" }`); with the `sql` feature the string fails to
+  parse and falls back to `Bare { table: "a." }`. Both lose the original reference.
+- Severity: low (empty identifier parts are degenerate), but it is a genuine contract break in
+  the quote/parse pair; any code persisting references via `to_quoted_string` silently changes
+  the reference. Not fixed per instructions; the general round-trip property excludes empty
+  parts with a comment referencing the pin.
+
+No other candidate bugs: the remaining 13 properties survived the 10x run under both feature
+configurations.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`next_up`/`next_down` == std everywhere**: unsound as stated — the implementation
+  intentionally steps through signed zeros one bit pattern at a time (`next_up(-0.0) == +0.0`,
+  not `TINY` as in std), and the crate's own unit tests pin that. The property was weakened to
+  "matches std except at ±0.0, where the exact documented stepping is asserted".
+- **`partial_cmp == Some(Equal)` implies `eq` across arbitrary type parameters**: unsound for
+  decimals — `partial_cmp` deliberately ignores precision (only scale must match, per the
+  comment about `add_checked` widening precision), while `eq` compares precision. The
+  implication is asserted only when both values share identical type parameters; the
+  `eq => Some(Equal)` direction is asserted unconditionally.
+- Decimal scale generation was constrained to `|scale| <= precision` and values to the
+  precision's digit budget — that protects arrow's documented `Decimal128` validity rules, not
+  the test's resources.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout (imperative draws, `one_of!`, `sampled_from`,
+  `optional`, `from_regex`, macro-in-test-fn pattern all worked first try); the only compile
+  friction was three `unused_imports` warnings from importing `Generator as _` in files that
+  never use combinator methods — the reference's note about importing the trait only when
+  needed is right, worth internalizing.
+- For huge workspaces, the "pick a small leaf member and scope everything with `-p`" strategy
+  worked well: the workspace-level toolchain pin downloaded automatically and iteration stayed
+  in the 5-30 s range. Worth a sentence in the skill's setup section: build feasibility in a
+  mega-workspace is a property of the *member*, not the workspace.
+- The skill's "pair no-panic/roundtrip with a validation/rejection check" and "generate at the
+  grammar's special characters" advice paid off directly: the table-reference bug is exactly a
+  degenerate-input quoting hole that uniform `text()` generation would rarely reach (found by
+  reasoning about the encode set, confirmed by the property).
+- `.hegel/` failure databases appear at the *crate* directory when running `-p` tests in a
+  workspace; the gitignore advice should mention workspaces (added `.hegel/` at workspace
+  root .gitignore, which covers nested dirs).
